[2026-10-16T12:05:30.119Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_01_ET_wvnTNhwbjDlubhdMXX064894","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"# 17. How do you interrupt a running thread?\n\n> **Section:** Thread Fundamentals · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/InterruptDemo.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\nCall `thread.interrupt()`. This sets the target's **interrupt status** flag. If the target is blocked in `sleep`, `wait`, `join`, or most interruptible I/O, it wakes up and throws `InterruptedException` (and the flag is cleared). If it is running, nothing happens immediately — the thread must **poll** `Thread.interrupted()` (clears the flag) or `isInterrupted()` (does not).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The two ways to observe an interrupt\n\n| Situation | Effect of `interrupt()` |\n| --- | --- |\n| Thread in `sleep/wait/join` | Throws `InterruptedException`; flag cleared |\n| Thread in interruptible I/O (NIO) | Throws `ClosedByInterruptException` |\n| Thread computing | Flag set; must be polled |\n\n### Clearing behaviour\n\n| Call | Clears flag? |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Thread.interrupted()` (static) | Yes |\n| `thread.isInterrupted()` (instance) | No |\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[thread.interrupt()] --> B{Thread blocked in sleep/wait/join?}\n    B -- Yes --> C[Throws InterruptedException, flag cleared]\n    B -- No --> D[Sets interrupt flag]\n    D --> E[Thread polls isInterrupted() / Thread.interrupted()]\n    C --> F[Handle: cleanup or re-interrupt]\n    E --> G[Exit loop gracefully]\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/InterruptDemo.java\nThread worker = new Thread(() -> {\n    while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {\n        System.out.println(\"Worker running...\");\n    }\n    System.out.println(\"Worker exiting gracefully\");\n});\nworker.start();\nThread.sleep(1600);   // let it run\nworker.interrupt();   // set the flag\nworker.join();\n```\n\n1. The loop condition polls `isInterrupted()`.\n2. `interrupt()` flips the flag.\n3. The worker falls out of the loop and exits cleanly.\n\nThe commented `Thread.sleep(500)` in the source shows the *blocking* case: interrupting would raise `InterruptedException`, which the handler should catch and re-interrupt.\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- `interrupt()` is Java's portable cancellation primitive — it is exactly what `Future.cancel(true)` and `shutdownNow()` use under the hood.\n- Prefer interrupt-driven cancellation for pooled/blocking work; reserve polling flags for tight compute loops.\n- Every long-running loop should include an interrupt check so cancellation latency is bounded.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ignoring the interrupt flag | Thread never stops | Poll or let it throw |\n| Swallowing `InterruptedException` | Loses the signal | Re-interrupt / propagate |\n| Assuming `interrupt()` stops compute loops | Flag alone does nothing | Check the flag in the loop |\n| Calling `interrupt()` on an unrelated thread | Confusing cancellation | Target only known workers |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** Does `interrupt()` immediately stop a thread?\n**A:** No — it signals; the thread must cooperate.\n\n**Q:** Difference between the two status methods?\n**A:** `Thread.interrupted()` clears the flag; `isInterrupted()` does not.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [18. How should code handle InterruptedException?](18.%20How%20should%20code%20handle%20InterruptedException.md)\n- [19. What is cooperative thread termination?](19.%20What%20is%20cooperative%20thread%20termination.md)\n","path":"questions/1. Thread Fundamentals/17. How do you interrupt a running thread.md"}}
