[2026-10-16T12:05:30.634Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_02_ET_2CexEOLLcDJL5Vp3wqMc1497","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"# 18. How should code handle InterruptedException?\n\n> **Section:** Thread Fundamentals · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/InterruptDemo.java`, `SpuriousWakeupDemo.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\nTreat `InterruptedException` as a **cancellation request**, not an error. Either **propagate** it (add `throws InterruptedException` up the stack) or **restore the interrupt status** with `Thread.currentThread().interrupt()` before returning. Never swallow it silently — doing so breaks cooperative cancellation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The three acceptable responses\n\n| Response | When | Snippet |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Propagate | Method can declare the exception | `void run() throws InterruptedException` |\n| Re-interrupt | Must catch, but can't propagate | `Thread.currentThread().interrupt();` |\n| Convert | Translate to a domain error | throw `new MyTaskCancelledException(e)` — keep the cause |\n\n### The anti-patterns\n\n- `catch (InterruptedException e) { }` — swallows cancellation.\n- `catch (InterruptedException e) { e.printStackTrace(); }` — logs but loses the signal.\n- Catching `Exception` broadly and ignoring it (as the commented-out code in `InterruptDemo` warns against).\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[InterruptedException thrown] --> B{Can propagate?}\n    B -- Yes --> C[declare throws, let caller decide]\n    B -- No --> D[restore flag]\n    D --> E[Thread.currentThread().interrupt]\n    B -- Domain error --> F[wrap and throw, keep cause]\n    C --> G[Cancellation propagates correctly]\n    E --> G\n    F --> G\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nCorrect propagation:\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/SpuriousWakeupDemo.java\ntry {\n    while (!ready) condition.await();\n    System.out.println(\"condition met, proceeding\");\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();   // restore status, then unwind\n} finally {\n    lock.unlock();\n}\n```\n\nIn `InterruptDemo` the intended handler (shown commented) re-interrupts after logging:\n\n```java\ncatch (InterruptedException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Worker interrupted during sleep\");\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();   // preserve the interrupt status\n}\n```\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- Establish a codebase rule: **`InterruptedException` must never be silently caught.** This is a repeatable lint/review item.\n- Restoring the flag is essential when you catch-but-can't-throw; otherwise upstream cancellation logic (e.g., `Future.cancel`) sees a \"completed normally\" task.\n- When translating to a domain exception, always set the cause (`new X(e)`) so the cancellation signal is not lost.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Empty catch block | Cancellation silently ignored | Re-interrupt / propagate |\n| Only logging it | Signal lost | Re-interrupt |\n| Not preserving the cause | Debugging dead-end | Pass `e` as cause |\n| Busy-waiting instead of interruption | Burns CPU | Use blocking + interrupt |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** What does `InterruptedException` really mean?\n**A:** Another thread requested this thread stop waiting and cancel.\n\n**Q:** What is the minimum correct catch block?\n**A:** `catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }`.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [17. How do you interrupt a running thread?](17.%20How%20do%20you%20interrupt%20a%20running%20thread.md)\n- [19. What is cooperative thread termination?](19.%20What%20is%20cooperative%20thread%20termination.md)\n","path":"questions/1. Thread Fundamentals/18. How should code handle InterruptedException.md"}}
