[2026-10-16T12:10:55.558Z] [TOOL_RESULT] {"id":"call_03_aCciQwEF2CKbL9Fq0QMT6513","name":"spawn_agent","elapsed_ms":124343,"ok":true,"output":"[Sub-agent result for: Write Section 3 tutorials Q51-60]\n(17 tool calls · 9 iter · 124.3s)\n\nRead-only mode is active, so I cannot use `write_file` directly. I'll deliver the complete file contents for all 10 files so the parent agent (or a write-enabled session) can persist them verbatim to the exact paths.\n\n---\n\n### FILE 1\n**Path:** `questions/3. Java Memory Model/51. Which operations are atomic in Java.md`\n\n````markdown\n# 51. Which operations are atomic in Java?\n\n> **Section:** Java Memory Model · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/javamemorymodel/MyRunnable.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\nIn Java, an **atomic** operation happens completely or not at all — no thread can observe an intermediate state. The JLS (17.6 / 17.7) guarantees that **reads and writes of `boolean`, `byte`, `char`, `short`, `int`, `float`, and reference variables are always atomic**, and since **Java 5** the same holds for `long` and `double` (even when non-`volatile`). Reference assignment is atomic even on 64-bit JVMs. What is **not** atomic is any **read-modify-write** compound (`i++`, `i += 1`, check-then-act) and **64-bit operations on `long`/`double` that are not single reads/writes** — which is exactly the class of bug `MyRunnable` triggers.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### What the JLS actually guarantees\n\n| Type / operand | Read atomic? | Write atomic? | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `boolean`, `byte`, `char`, `short`, `int`, `float` | Yes | Yes | Always, since Java 1.0 |\n| `long`, `double` | Yes | Yes | Guaranteed atomic **since Java 5** (JLS 17.7) |\n| Object reference | Yes | Yes | Assignment of a reference is atomic (no torn pointers) |\n| `i++`, `i += n`, `--i` | No | No | Read-modify-write = 3+ bytecodes |\n| `i = i + 1` | No | No | Same as above |\n| Check-then-act (`if (x) y`) | No | No | Race window between the two operations |\n| `long`/`double` **non-volatile, pre-Java-5** | No | No | Could tear into two 32-bit halves |\n\n### Atomicity ≠ thread safety\n\nAtomicity only mea"}
