[2026-10-16T12:12:01.953Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_00_kt4omXF7QISFynFVtsUu6018","name":"write_file","args":{"path":"questions/2. Synchronization and Locks/21. How does the synchronized keyword work internally.md","content":"# 21. How does the synchronized keyword work internally?\n\n> **Section:** Synchronization and Locks · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/synchronizekeyword/App.java`, `src/com/java/javasynchronize/SynchronizedExchanger.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\n`synchronized` acquires a **monitor** (intrinsic lock) attached to an object. On entry the JVM emits a `monitorenter` (JVM bytecode) and on exit a `monitorexit`, emitted in a `try/finally` so the lock is always released even if an exception is thrown. The monitor gives mutual exclusion **and** a happens-before edge (unlock → subsequent lock), so it guarantees visibility as well as atomicity.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### What the compiler emits\n\n| Source | Bytecode |\n| --- | --- |\n| `synchronized (obj) { ... }` | `monitorenter` … `monitorexit` (normal exit) + `monitorexit` (exceptional exit) |\n| `synchronized` instance method | Method flag `ACC_SYNCHRONIZED` (implicit monitor on `this`) |\n| `synchronized static` method | `ACC_SYNCHRONIZED` (implicit monitor on the `Class` object) |\n\n### The lock object\n\n- Instance method → monitor of `this`.\n- Static method / `synchronized (Foo.class)` → monitor of the `Class` object.\n- Block → monitor of the expression object.\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant T1 as Thread 1\n    participant M as Monitor(obj)\n    participant T2 as Thread 2\n    T1->>M: monitorenter (acquire)\n    T2->>M: monitorenter (BLOCKED, queued)\n    T1->>M: monitorexit (release + happens-before)\n    M-->>T2: granted (RUNNABLE)\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/synchronizekeyword/App.java\npublic class App {\n    private int count = 0;\n    public synchronized void increment() {   // implicit monitor = this\n        count++;\n    }\n    public void doWork() {\n        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> { for (int i=0;i<10000;i++) increment(); });\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> { for (int i=0;i<10000;i++) increment(); });\n        t1.start(); t2.start();\n        // join + print -> always 20000\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`SynchronizedExchanger` shows both forms locking the *same* monitor:\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/javasynchronize/SynchronizedExchanger.java\npublic synchronized void setObject(Object o) { this.object = o; }   // this\npublic void setObj(Object o) { synchronized (this) { this.object = o; } }  // this\n```\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- Locks are associated with **objects**, not code — two threads must synchronize on the *same* object for mutual exclusion.\n- Modern JVMs use biased/thin/inflated lock states and may optimise uncontended locks away (lock elision) — but never rely on that for correctness.\n- Never lock on public objects, `String` literals, or boxed primitives: they can be shared by unrelated code and cause surprising contention or deadlock.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Locking on a `String`/boxed value | Interned and shared → cross-talk | Lock a dedicated `private final Object` |\n| Different lock objects | No mutual exclusion | Share one monitor |\n| Locking `this` in a public API | External code can lock it | Use a private lock object |\n| Holding the lock during I/O | Serialises everything | Minimise critical sections |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** Why is `synchronized` also a visibility guarantee?\n**A:** Unlock happens-before the next lock on the same monitor, flushing/reloading state.\n\n**Q:** What bytecode does a synchronized block compile to?\n**A:** `monitorenter`/`monitorexit` pairs, with the exit duplicated into the exception path.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [22. What is a monitor in Java concurrency?](22.%20What%20is%20a%20monitor%20in%20Java%20concurrency.md)\n- [23. What are intrinsic locks in Java?](23.%20What%20are%20intrinsic%20locks%20in%20Java.md)\n"}}
