[2026-10-16T12:12:01.954Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_01_7W8BkcrfUVFwdYEjJL6C6078","name":"write_file","args":{"path":"questions/2. Synchronization and Locks/22. What is a monitor in Java concurrency.md","content":"# 22. What is a monitor in Java concurrency?\n\n> **Section:** Synchronization and Locks · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/javasynchronize/SynchronizedExchanger.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\nA **monitor** is the synchronization construct behind `synchronized`. In Java every object is associated with a monitor that provides (1) **mutual exclusion** — only one thread holds it at a time — and (2) **wait/notify coordination** — threads can `wait()`, `notify()`, and `notifyAll()` via the monitor. It is the concrete realisation of the classic Hoare/Mesa monitor concept.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Monitor responsibilities\n\n| Capability | Mechanism |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mutual exclusion | `monitorenter` / `monitorexit` |\n| Ownership | The lock is *owned* (re-entrantly) by the holding thread |\n| Wait set | Threads blocked in `Object.wait()` |\n| Entry set | Threads `BLOCKED` waiting to acquire |\n| Signalling | `notify()` / `notifyAll()` move a thread from the wait set to the entry set |\n\n### Ownership is re-entrant\n\nA thread that holds the monitor can re-enter it (see `Reentrance`). The monitor keeps an ownership count, not a binary flag.\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> EntrySet: monitorenter, lock busy\n    EntrySet --> Owner: lock released\n    Owner --> WaitSet: wait() (release + park)\n    WaitSet --> EntrySet: notify()/notifyAll()\n    Owner --> [*]: monitorexit\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/javasynchronize/SynchronizedExchanger.java\npublic synchronized void setObject(Object o) { this.object = o; }\npublic synchronized Object getObject()  { return this.object; }\n```\n\nBoth methods share the monitor of `this`. A caller blocked in `getObject()` waits in the **entry set** until `setObject()` releases the monitor.\n\nWaiting / signalling (monitor wait set):\n\n```java\nsynchronized (lock) {\n    while (!ready) lock.wait();   // enters wait set, releases monitor\n}\n```\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- The wait set is *not* FIFO-fair; `notify()` picks an arbitrary waiter — use `notifyAll()` or a `Condition`/`BlockingQueue` for predictable designs.\n- Because monitoring is per-object, a class that synchronizes on `this` exposes its monitor to all callers; prefer a private lock object for libraries.\n- Higher-level utilities (`ReentrantLock`, `Semaphore`, `BlockingQueue`) build on the same ideas but add fairness, timeouts, and multiple conditions.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Calling `wait()` outside `synchronized` | `IllegalMonitorStateException` | Hold the monitor first |\n| Using `notify()` with multiple waiters | Wrong thread may wake | Use `notifyAll()` |\n| Assuming fairness | Monitor is not fair | Use `ReentrantLock(true)` |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** What two things does a monitor provide?\n**A:** Mutual exclusion and wait/notify coordination.\n\n**Q:** Is a monitor fair?\n**A:** No — acquisition order is unspecified.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [21. How does the synchronized keyword work internally?](21.%20How%20does%20the%20synchronized%20keyword%20work%20internally.md)\n- [32. How do condition variables work in Java?](32.%20How%20do%20condition%20variables%20work%20in%20Java.md)\n"}}
[2026-10-16T12:12:01.954Z] [TOOL_RESULT] {"id":"call_01_7W8BkcrfUVFwdYEjJL6C6078","name":"write_file","elapsed_ms":0,"ok":true,"output":"PLAN_MODE_FORBIDDEN: write_file is a write/exec tool and is disabled in Plan mode. Stay read-only (read_file, grep_search, list_dir, find_files, web_search, web_fetch, update_plan) and produce a plan for the user to review. The user can switch to Agent mode to execute it."}
[2026-10-16T12:12:01.955Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_02_7Pt5lAq8C6q0GujNAiBF9939","name":"write_file","args":{"path":"questions/2. Synchronization and Locks/23. What are intrinsic locks in Java.md","content":"# 23. What are intrinsic locks in Java?\n\n> **Section:** Synchronization and Locks · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/synchronizekeyword/App.java`, `src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/StaticSyncDemo.java`, `NonStaticSyncDemo.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\nAn **intrinsic lock** (a.k.a. *monitor lock* or *intrinsic monitor*) is the built-in lock every Java object carries and that `synchronized` uses. It is **re-entrant**, **non-interruptible** while blocked, offers **no timeout**, and is **implicitly released** when a synchronized block/method exits (including via exception). The explicit alternative is `java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock`.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Which lock does a method use?\n\n| Method form | Intrinsic lock |\n| --- | --- |\n| `synchronized void m()` | `this` |\n| `synchronized static void m()` | `Foo.class` |\n| `synchronized (obj) {}` | `obj` |\n\n### Intrinsic vs explicit lock\n\n| Property | Intrinsic (`synchronized`) | Explicit (`ReentrantLock`) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Re-entrant | Yes | Yes |\n| Fairness | No | Optional (`true`) |\n| `tryLock` / timeout | No | Yes |\n| Interruptible wait | No | `lockInterruptibly()` |\n| Multiple conditions | No (single wait set) | Yes (`newCondition()`) |\n| Release | Automatic | Manual (`finally { unlock }`) |\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Object] --> L[Intrinsic lock / monitor]\n    L --> I[\"Instance method -> this\"]\n    L --> S[\"Static method -> Foo.class\"]\n    L --> B[\"Block -> named object\"]\n    L --> R[Re-entrant]\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nInstance vs class monitor — `StaticSyncDemo` and `NonStaticSyncDemo` show why the choice matters:\n\n```java\n// Non-static: c1 and c2 each have their OWN monitor -> no mutual exclusion\nCounter c1 = new Counter(); Counter c2 = new Counter();\nnew Thread(() -> c1.increment()).start();\nnew Thread(() -> c2.increment()).start();   // can run concurrently\n\n// Static: one monitor shared by the whole class\npublic static synchronized void increment() { count++; }\n```\n\nSo `StaticCounter` serialises across *all* instances, while per-instance `Counter` only serialises within one object.\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- Prefer intrinsic locks for simple, short, strictly scoped critical sections — they cannot leak (auto-release) and are JVM-optimised.\n- Reach for `ReentrantLock` when you need timeouts, interruptibility, fairness, or multiple wait conditions.\n- Watch for **lock granularity**: `StaticSyncDemo` (class lock) can become a global bottleneck; a per-instance or per-key lock scales better.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Assuming instance lock covers static data | Different monitor | Sync on the class |\n| Deadlock via nested intrinsic locks | No timeout to escape | Consistent ordering / `tryLock` |\n| Blocking forever | No interruption | Use `Lock.lockInterruptibly` |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** Is the intrinsic lock fair?\n**A:** No.\n\n**Q:** Can you interrupt a thread blocked on an intrinsic lock?\n**A:** No — use an explicit `Lock` for interruptibility.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [24. How are intrinsic locks different from explicit locks?](24.%20How%20are%20intrinsic%20locks%20different%20from%20explicit%20locks.md)\n- [40. When should you prefer locks over synchronized blocks?](40.%20When%20should%20you%20prefer%20locks%20over%20synchronized%20blocks.md)\n"}}
