[2026-10-16T12:12:01.955Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_03_TkyqdKswx94Fj1nZn4ON4987","name":"write_file","args":{"path":"questions/2. Synchronization and Locks/24. How are intrinsic locks different from explicit locks.md","content":"# 24. How are intrinsic locks different from explicit locks?\n\n> **Section:** Synchronization and Locks · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/synchronizekeyword/App.java` (intrinsic) vs `src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/ReentrantLockDemo.java` (explicit)\n\n## TL;DR\n\nIntrinsic locks (`synchronized`) are **implicit, auto-released, non-interruptible, and unfair**. Explicit locks (`java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock`, e.g. `ReentrantLock`) are **manual, must be released in `finally`, support interruption, timeouts, fairness, and multiple conditions**. Use intrinsic locks by default; switch to explicit locks when you need those extra capabilities.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n| Capability | `synchronized` | `ReentrantLock` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Acquire/release | Implicit | `lock()` / `unlock()` in `finally` |\n| Fairness | No | Constructor flag |\n| `tryLock()` / timeout | No | Yes |\n| Interruptible acquire | No | `lockInterruptibly()` |\n| Multiple wait sets | No | `newCondition()` |\n| Read/write split | No | `ReentrantReadWriteLock` |\n| Risk | Low (auto-release) | Forget `unlock()` → deadlock |\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Intrinsic\n        A[synchronized] --> A1[auto unlock]\n        A[synchronized] --> A2[no timeout/fairness]\n    end\n    subgraph Explicit\n        B[ReentrantLock.lock] --> B1[unlock in finally]\n        B --> B2[tryLock / timeout / fair]\n        B --> B3[Condition]\n    end\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nExplicit lock — note the mandatory `finally`:\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/ReentrantLockDemo.java\nprivate final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock(true); // fair\npublic void increment() {\n    lock.lock();\n    try { counter++; }\n    finally { lock.unlock(); }     // MUST always unlock\n}\n```\n\nIntrinsic version — smaller and leak-proof:\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/synchronizekeyword/App.java\npublic synchronized void increment() { count++; }\n```\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- Default to `synchronized`: it cannot leak the lock and the JVM optimises it well.\n- Use `ReentrantLock` for **timeouts** (`tryLock(t, u)`) — a key defence against deadlocks in large systems — or when you need fairness/conditions.\n- A common production bug is forgetting `unlock()` in an exception path; always use `try/finally`.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Missing `unlock()` | Permanent deadlock | `try { ... } finally { unlock(); }` |\n| Using explicit lock for simple cases | Extra complexity/risk | Prefer `synchronized` |\n| Assuming fairness by default | Not fair unless set | `new ReentrantLock(true)` |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** What do explicit locks add over `synchronized`?\n**A:** Fairness, timeouts, interruptible acquisition, multiple conditions, read/write separation.\n\n**Q:** Which should be the default?\n**A:** `synchronized`, unless a listed capability is required.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [23. What are intrinsic locks in Java?](23.%20What%20are%20intrinsic%20locks%20in%20Java.md)\n- [27. How do you use tryLock() with timeouts?](27.%20How%20do%20you%20use%20tryLock()%20with%20timeouts.md)\n"}}
