[2026-10-16T12:12:01.956Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_04_ouksKxQGSHlYPASMsc4j8059","name":"write_file","args":{"path":"questions/2. Synchronization and Locks/25. What is a reentrant lock.md","content":"# 25. What is a reentrant lock?\n\n> **Section:** Synchronization and Locks · **Level:** Architect / Tech Lead · **Related code:** `src/com/java/javasynchronize/Reentrance.java`, `src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/ReentrantLockDemo.java`\n\n## TL;DR\n\nA **re-entrant** lock lets the *same* thread acquire it again while it already holds it — without deadlocking on itself. Java's intrinsic lock and `ReentrantLock` are both re-entrant. The lock tracks an **ownership count**; the thread must release it the same number of times it acquired it. This is what allows a synchronized method to call another synchronized method on the same object.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Why re-entrancy matters\n\n```java\npublic synchronized void inc()      { count++; }\npublic synchronized int incAndGet() { inc(); return count; }  // re-enters same monitor\n```\n\nWithout re-entrancy, `incAndGet()` holding the monitor and calling `inc()` (which wants the same monitor) would **self-deadlock**.\n\n### The hold count\n\n| Acquisition | Hold count |\n| --- | --- |\n| `lock.lock()` | 1 |\n| nested `lock.lock()` | 2 |\n| `lock.unlock()` | 1 |\n| `lock.unlock()` | 0 → released |\n\n## Diagram\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant T as Thread\n    participant L as ReentrantLock\n    T->>L: lock()  (count=1)\n    T->>L: lock()  (count=2, same thread OK)\n    T->>L: unlock() (count=1)\n    T->>L: unlock() (count=0, released)\n    Note over L: a DIFFERENT thread would block at lock()\n```\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/javasynchronize/Reentrance.java\npublic synchronized void inc() { this.count++; }\npublic synchronized int incAndGet() {\n    inc();              // re-enters the monitor of 'this'\n    return this.count;\n}\n```\n\nWith an explicit lock the same behaviour applies — note `getHoldCount()`:\n\n```java\n// src/com/java/multithreading/interview/questions/ReentrantLockDemo.java\nlock.lock();\ntry { /* re-entrant; getHoldCount() reflects depth */ }\nfinally { lock.unlock(); }\n```\n\n## Architect / Tech-Lead Insights\n\n- Re-entrancy can mask designs that hold a lock too long; if hold counts grow, the critical section is probably too large.\n- It only helps the *same* thread. Two different threads still serialize.\n- `ReentrantReadWriteLock` also has re-entrant read/write semantics with subtle upgrade/downgrade rules (downgrade is allowed; upgrading read→write is not).\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it hurts | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unlocking fewer times than locked | Lock never released | Match unlock count / use `try/finally` |\n| Assuming cross-thread re-entry | Only same thread | Redesign |\n| Try to upgrade read→write lock | Deadlocks | Release read, take write |\n\n## Interview Q&A\n\n**Q:** Is `synchronized` re-entrant?\n**A:** Yes — nested synchronized methods on the same object work.\n\n**Q:** What tracks re-entrancy?\n**A:** An ownership count on the lock.\n\n## Related Questions\n\n- [26. How does fairness policy affect ReentrantLock?](26.%20How%20does%20fairness%20policy%20affect%20ReentrantLock.md)\n- [29. How does ReentrantReadWriteLock improve concurrency?](29.%20How%20does%20ReentrantReadWriteLock%20improve%20concurrency.md)\n"}}
